---
import { existsSync, readFileSync } from 'node:fs';
import { resolve } from 'node:path';
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
import extension from '../../../extension/package.json';
const filename = `formpilot-${extension.version}-chrome.zip`;
// Astro executes the bundled page from its output directory; the workspace cwd stays stable.
const downloads = resolve('public/downloads');
const available = existsSync(resolve(downloads, filename));
const release = available
  ? (JSON.parse(readFileSync(resolve(downloads, 'release.json'), 'utf8')) as {
      bytes: number;
      sha256: string;
    })
  : undefined;
---

<Layout
  title="Download and install FormPilot"
  description="Download the prebuilt FormPilot extension for desktop Chrome and Chromium browsers. Extract, load unpacked and start reviewing forms. No build tools needed."
>
  <div class="page-heading">
    <h1>Your next form starts here.</h1>
    <p>
      Download FormPilot, add it to your browser, and save your first profile. No account or build
      tools needed.
    </p>
  </div>
  <article class="prose">
    <section class="panel" aria-labelledby="download-title" style="padding:24px;margin-bottom:32px">
      <span class="pill">Desktop Chromium · v{extension.version}</span>
      <h2 id="download-title" style="margin-top:16px">
        Ready to add to Chrome
      </h2>
      <p>
        A small ZIP containing the complete extension. Extract it, then use your browser’s Load
        unpacked option.
      </p>
      {available && release ? (
        <>
          <p>
            <a class="button" href={`/downloads/${filename}`} download={filename}>
              Download FormPilot v{extension.version}
            </a>
          </p>
          <p class="small muted">
            ZIP · {Math.round(release.bytes / 1024)} KB · No Node.js or npm required
          </p>
          <p class="small">
            <a href="/downloads/INSTALL.txt" download>
              Installation instructions
            </a>{' '}
            ·{' '}
            <a href="/downloads/SHA256SUMS.txt" download>
              SHA-256 checksum
            </a>
          </p>
          <details>
            <summary>Verify this download</summary>
            <p class="small">
              Compare this SHA-256 checksum with the downloaded ZIP. It checks file integrity; it is
              not a browser-store signature.
            </p>
            <code style="overflow-wrap:anywhere;word-break:break-all">{release.sha256}</code>
          </details>
        </>
      ) : (
        <p>
          The package has not been generated in this source checkout. Run{' '}
          <code>npm run package:extension</code>, or follow the source-build instructions below.
        </p>
      )}
    </section>

    <h2>Install in four steps</h2>
    <ol>
      <li>
        <strong>Extract the ZIP.</strong> Keep the extracted folder somewhere permanent, such as
        Documents/FormPilot. The folder must contain <code>manifest.json</code>.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Open the extensions page.</strong> Type <code>chrome://extensions</code> in Chrome’s
        address bar. Use <code>edge://extensions</code> for Edge or <code>brave://extensions</code>{' '}
        for Brave.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Turn on Developer mode.</strong> Click <strong>Load unpacked</strong> and select the
        extracted folder, not the ZIP file.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Pin FormPilot and save a profile.</strong> Choose Create your first profile, or try
        the fictional sample in profile settings.
      </li>
    </ol>
    <p>
      This follows the documented{' '}
      <a href="https://developer.chrome.com/docs/extensions/get-started/tutorial/hello-world#load-unpacked">
        Chrome
      </a>{' '}
      and{' '}
      <a href="https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/microsoft-edge/extensions/getting-started/extension-sideloading">
        Edge
      </a>{' '}
      installation flows. Browser or organization policies may disable Developer mode.
    </p>

    <h2>Which browsers can use it?</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>Chrome / Chromium:</strong> the tested build target. Chrome 120+ is the declared API
        minimum; the exact tested version is recorded in the repository’s QA report.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Edge and Brave:</strong> the same Chromium package is intended for these browsers,
        but they have not been independently tested here.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Firefox, Safari and mobile browsers:</strong> this package does not support them.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      There is no store listing yet. This download needs the manual steps above. One-click
      installation and automatic store updates require a published browser-store release.
    </p>

    <h2>Try your first reviewed fill</h2>
    <p>
      Use the fictional sample profile on the <a href="/forms/event/">event registration demo</a>.
      Click the FormPilot toolbar icon, then <strong>Review this page → Scan page</strong>. Review
      the suggestions and choose <strong>Fill selected fields</strong>. The form stays unsubmitted.
    </p>
    <p>
      Edit one filled field, then undo: your later edit should remain untouched.{' '}
      <a href="/demo/">Try the interactive playground</a> if you want to explore before installing.
    </p>

    <h2>Updating or removing</h2>
    <p>
      Keep the extracted folder in place. To update, export a profile backup, replace the files in
      that same folder, click Reload on the extension’s settings card, and refresh your form tab.
      Reopen review from the toolbar. Do not remove the installed extension first: moving to a new
      folder can create a different extension identity and separate storage.
    </p>
    <p>
      Unpacked builds do not update automatically. Removing the extension normally removes its
      browser-local data. Exported profile files remain wherever you saved them and contain readable
      personal information.
    </p>

    <h2>Prefer to build from source?</h2>
    <p>Use Node 22.22.x or 24 LTS and npm 10+. From the repository root:</p>
    <pre><code>npm ci{ '\n' }npm run build</code></pre>
    <p>
      This creates the unpacked extension at <code>apps/extension/.output/chrome-mv3/</code>, the
      shareable ZIP and checksum at <code>dist/releases/</code>, and the website at{' '}
      <code>apps/website/dist/</code>. To generate just the extension package, run{' '}
      <code>npm run package:extension</code>. Nothing is published by either command.
    </p>
    <p>
      Read <a href="/privacy/">privacy</a> and{' '}
      <a href="/limitations/">troubleshooting and limitations</a> before filling details into a
      website.
    </p>
  </article>
</Layout>
